# Line-ending normalisation.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so without this file a Windows
# clone checks out every shell script as CRLF. Bash tolerates that — sync.sh runs
# and the status line renders — but shellcheck does not: scripts/lint-shell.sh
# reported 471 x SC1017 (literal carriage return) and exited 1 on a Windows
# clone, burying the genuine findings under cascading phantom parse errors. A
# Windows contributor could not pass the project's own gate.
#
# Store everything as LF in the repo and check text out as LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executed by bash. Explicit rather than relying on the wildcard above, because
# this is the case that actually broke.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# PowerShell 7 reads LF fine; keep it consistent with the rest of the tree.
*.ps1   text eol=lf

# Batch files are the one real exception: CRLF is load-bearing, as labels and
# goto can misbehave with LF endings.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
